Given the following four conclusions:  
\\((1)\\) The center of symmetry of the function \\(f(x)= \dfrac {x-1}{2x+1}\\) is \\((- \dfrac {1}{2},- \dfrac {1}{2})\\);  
\\((2)\\) If the equation in \\(x\\), \\(x- \dfrac {1}{x}+k=0\\), has no real roots for \\(x \in (0,1)\\), then the range of \\(k\\) is \\(k \geqslant 2\\);  
\\((3)\\) Given that point \\(P(a,b)\\) and point \\(Q(1,0)\\) are on opposite sides of the line \\(2x-3y+1=0\\), then \\(3b-2a > 1\\);  
\\((4)\\) If the graph of the function \\(f(x)=\sin (2x- \dfrac {\pi}{3})\\) is shifted to the right by \\(\varphi (\varphi > 0)\\) units and becomes an even function, then the minimum value of \\(\varphi\\) is \\( \dfrac {\pi}{12}\\),  
among these conclusions, the correct ones are: \_\_\_\_\_\_ .

s:
Solution:  
\\((1)\\) The function \\(f(x)= \dfrac {x-1}{2x+1}= \dfrac {1}{2}+ \dfrac {- \dfrac {3}{2}}{2x+1}\\) has its center of symmetry at \\((- \dfrac {1}{2}, \dfrac {1}{2})\\), which is incorrect;  
\\((2)\\) If the equation in \\(x\\), \\(x- \dfrac {1}{x}+k=0\\), has no real roots for \\(x \in (0,1)\\), then \\(k= \dfrac {1}{x}-x\\) has no real roots for \\(x \in (0,1)\\), so the range of \\(k\\) is \\(k \leqslant 0\\), which is incorrect;  
\\((3)\\) Given that point \\(P(a,b)\\) and point \\(Q(1,0)\\) are on opposite sides of the line \\(2x-3y+1=0\\), then \\(2a-3b+1 < 0\\), so \\(3b-2a > 1\\), which is correct;  
\\((4)\\) If the graph of the function \\(f(x)=\sin (2x- \dfrac {\pi}{3})\\) is shifted to the right by \\(\varphi (\varphi > 0)\\) units and becomes an even function, the resulting function is \\(y=\sin [2(x-\varphi)- \dfrac {\pi}{3}]=\sin (2x- \dfrac {\pi}{3}-2\varphi)=\cos (2x- \dfrac {5\pi}{6}-2\varphi)\\), which is symmetric about the \\(y\\)-axis. Then \\(- \dfrac {5\pi}{6}-2\varphi=k\pi\\), \\(k \in \mathbb{Z}\\), i.e., \\(\varphi=- \dfrac {1}{2}k\pi- \dfrac {5\pi}{12}\\), \\(k \in \mathbb{Z}\\). When \\(k=-1\\), the minimum value of \\(\varphi\\) is \\( \dfrac {\pi}{12}\\), which is correct.  
Therefore, the answer is: $\boxed{(3)(4)}$.  
Based on the properties of inverse proportion functions and the rules of graphical transformations, we can judge the truth of \\((1)\\); by using the relationship between the roots of equations and the zeros of functions, and graphical methods, it's easy to judge the truth of \\((2)\\); by understanding the positional relationship between points and lines on a plane, we can derive the inequalities that \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must satisfy, thus judging the truth of \\((3)\\); by knowing the symmetry of sine functions and the graphical transformations, we can judge the truth of \\((4)\\).  
This question examines knowledge of graphical transformations, symmetry properties of functions, applications of simple linear programming, and the relationship between the roots of equations and the zeros of functions. Mastering the corresponding basic knowledge points and their application is key to solving this question.

